github_url: "https://github.com/unbraind/pm-graph/issues/50"
body: "`test/id-resolution.test.ts:59` — *\"path resolves source/target by unique prefix and case-insensitive id\"* — can fail in its **setup**, for reasons unrelated to the code under test.\n\n## Observed\n\nCI run [30797275564](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-graph/actions/runs/30797275564), `test (26)` only, on a branch whose only source change was a lockfile refresh:\n\n```\n✖ path resolves source/target by unique prefix and case-insensitive id\n  AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: test setup expected a unique non-exact prefix\n  actual: null, expected: true\n```\n\n`test (22)` passed on the same commit, and three earlier runs on the same branch passed.\n\n## Why it happens\n\nThe test creates three items, then calls `uniqueNonExactPrefix(c, [a, b, c])`, which walks\nprefixes of `c` of length `1 .. len-1` and returns the first matching exactly one id. It\nreturns `null` when **no proper prefix of `c` is unique**.\n\nIds are `pm-` plus four random base36 characters, so the last usable prefix is `pm-` plus\nthree of them. `null` therefore occurs whenever another of the three ids shares `c`'s first\nthree random characters. Verified the generator is uniformly distributed (60 real ids, 30–32\ndistinct values per position), so this is genuine chance rather than a biased generator:\n\n```\n3 pairs x (1/36)^3  ≈  6.4e-5 per run\n```\n\nSimulated over 400,000 trials: **16 failures (0.004%)**. Low, but it is a coin flip on every\nrun of every PR across the fleet, and it landed today.\n\n## Why it is worth fixing rather than re-running\n\nThe assertion is on the *fixture*, not on `pm-graph`. When it trips, a red gate means \"the\nrandom ids were unlucky\", which is indistinguishable at a glance from a real regression in\nprefix resolution — so the failure mode actively erodes trust in the one gate that covers\nthis behaviour. A gate that can fail without a defect is the same problem as one that can\npass with a defect.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nMake the fixture deterministic instead of asserting that chance was kind — e.g. regenerate\nthe workspace (bounded attempts) until the three ids admit a unique non-exact prefix, then\nassert. That preserves exactly what the test intends to exercise.\n\n**One caveat for whoever takes it.** `uniqueNonExactPrefix` computes uniqueness against\n`[a, b, c]`, but `pm-graph`'s resolver resolves against **every item in the workspace**. Those\ncoincide only because the fixture has exactly three items. Any fix that adds items to the\nworkspace to obtain a usable prefix would break that assumption and could make the test assert\na prefix the resolver considers ambiguous. The uniqueness computation should be widened to all\nworkspace ids at the same time.\n\nFound while landing the fleet-wide `pm-changelog` release-date refresh (PR #49); not caused by\nit, and not fixed there to keep a dependency bump from carrying an unrelated test change."
